  "configuration_id": "config_025",
  "reason": "Evidence: Both completed experiments (config_001: 0.639, config_006: 0.635) use pca_n2000 as the RNA encoder with avgpool aggregation and weighted fusion, differing only in image encoder (resnet50 vs vit_l) with a negligible ~0.4% gap. This gives a baseline of ~0.635-0.639 but tells us nothing about whether the RNA representation is a bottleneck. Question: config_025 changes only the RNA encoder from pca_n2000 (linear, 2000-dim) to scvi_n200 (learned, 200-dim) while keeping resnet50, avgpool, weighted fusion, and multiprofile identical to the best config (config_001). This is a clean single-variable test of whether a learned, biologically-informed RNA representation outperforms a simple linear projection. Rationale: The RNA encoder is the most fundamental untested design axis; if scvi_n200 improves accuracy, it opens a rich exploitation path (scvi + transformer aggregation, scvi + vit_l, etc.). If it doesn't, we can rule out RNA representation as the bottleneck and focus on aggregation/fusion. With 7 experiments remaining, resolving this high-uncertainty dimension early is the best use of the next slot. Expected cost: scvi_n200 produces a 200-dim representation (vs 2000 for PCA), so the downstream multimodal model is smaller; I estimate ~8-10 GPU hours, roughly comparable to or slightly below config_001's 10.60."
}

